{"type": "step", "node_id": 24, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "## Solution\n\n**Setting up coordinates.** Place $B=(0,0)$ and $C=(14,0)$. From $AB=13$, $AC=15$:\n\n$$x^2+y^2=169,\\quad (x-14)^2+y^2=225 \\implies x=5,\\; y=12.$$\n\nSo $A=(5,12)$, $B=(0,0)$, $C=(14,0)$.\n\n**Centroid and point Y.** The centroid is $G=\\left(\\frac{19}{3}, 4\\right)$. Since $G$ lies on the median from $A$ to the midpoint of $BC$, the line $AG$ meets $BC$ at $Y=(7,0)$.\n\n**Reflecting G across the sides.**\n\n- **Across $BC$ (the $x$-axis):** $G_A = \\left(\\frac{19}{3}, -4\\right)$.\n\n- **Across $AC$:** The line $AC$ has equation $4x+3y-56=0$. Using the reflection formula with $a^2+b^2=25$:\n$$d = \\frac{4\\cdot\\frac{19}{3}+3\\cdot 4-56}{25} = -\\frac{56}{75},$$\n$$G_B = \\left(\\frac{19}{3}+\\frac{448}{75},\\; 4+\\frac{336}{75}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{923}{75},\\; \\frac{212}{25}\\right).$$\n\n- **Across $AB$:** The line $AB$ has equation $12x-5y=0$, with $a^2+b^2=169$:\n$$d = \\frac{12\\cdot\\frac{19}{3}-5\\cdot 4}{169} = \\frac{56}{169},$$\n$$G_C = \\left(\\frac{19}{3}-\\frac{1344}{169},\\; 4+\\frac{560}{169}\\right) = \\left(-\\frac{821}{507},\\; \\frac{1236}{169}\\right).$$\n\n**Circumcenter $O_G=(u,v)$ of $\\triangle G_A G_B G_C$.** Setting $|O_G-G_A|^2=|O_G-G_C|^2$:\n\n$$2u\\!\\left(-\\frac{1344}{169}\\right)+2v\\!\\left(\\frac{1912}{169}\\right)=0 \\implies -1344u+1912v=0 \\implies v=\\frac{168}{239}\\,u.$$\n\nSetting $|O_G-G_A|^2=|O_G-G_B|^2$ yields (after simplification):\n\n$$56u+117v=784.$$\n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{168}{239}u$:\n\n$$56u+\\frac{19656}{239}u=784 \\implies \\frac{33040}{239}u=784 \\implies u=\\frac{784\\cdot 239}{33040}=\\frac{1673}{295},$$\n$$v=\\frac{168}{239}\\cdot\\frac{1673}{295}=\\frac{1176}{295}.$$\n\nSo $O_G=\\left(\\frac{1673}{295},\\frac{1176}{295}\\right)$.\n\n**Point X: line $A\\,O_G$ meets $BC$ ($y=0$).** The direction from $A$ to $O_G$ is:\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{1673}{295}-5,\\;\\frac{1176}{295}-12\\right)=\\left(\\frac{198}{295},\\;-\\frac{2364}{295}\\right).$$\n\nParametrize: $P(t)=A+t\\cdot\\vec{d}$. Setting $y=0$:\n\n$$12-\\frac{2364}{295}\\,t=0 \\implies t=\\frac{295}{197}.$$\n\n$$x = 5+\\frac{198}{295}\\cdot\\frac{295}{197}=5+\\frac{198}{197}=\\frac{1183}{197}.$$\n\nSo $X=\\left(\\frac{1183}{197},0\\right)$.\n\n**Distance $XY$:**\n\n$$XY = 7-\\frac{1183}{197}=\\frac{1379-1183}{197}=\\frac{196}{197}.$$\n\nSince $197$ is prime and $\\gcd(196,197)=1$, the fraction is irreducible. Thus $a=196$, $b=197$.\n\n$$a+b = \\boxed{393}.$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this problem. I need to find the distance XY on line BC, where X is where line A-O_G meets BC, and Y is where line A-G meets BC (which is the median from A).\n\nLet me set up coordinates. Triangle ABC with AB=13, BC=14, CA=15.\n\nLet me place B and C on the x-axis. Let B=(0,0), C=(14,0). Find A.\nAB=13: A is distance 13 from B.\nAC=15: A is distance 15 from C.\nx² + y² = 169\n(x-14)² + y² = 225\nx² - 28x + 196 + y² = 225\n169 - 28x + 196 = 225\n365 - 28x = 225\n28x = 140\nx = 5\ny² = 169 - 25 = 144, y = 12.\nSo A=(5,12), B=(0,0), C=(14,0).\n\nCentroid G = ((5+0+14)/3, (12+0+0)/3) = (19/3, 4).\n\nY is where line AG meets BC (y=0). Line AG: from A=(5,12) to G=(19/3,4).\nActually, the centroid lies on the median, so line AG is the median from A to midpoint of BC. Midpoint of BC = (7,0). So Y = (7,0).\n\nIndeed, G is on the median from A, so Y = midpoint of BC = (7,0).\n\nNow G_A is reflection of G across BC. BC is the x-axis (y=0). So G_A = (19/3, -4).\n\nG_B is reflection of G across AC. \nG_C is reflection of G across AB.\n\nO_G is equidistant from G_A, G_B, G_C — it's the circumcenter of triangle G_A G_B G_C.\n\nThen X is where line A-O_G meets BC (y=0).\n\nLet me compute G_B and G_C.\n\nLine AC: from A=(5,12) to C=(14,0). Direction (9,-12), or (3,-4). Normal (4,3). Line: 4(x-5) + 3(y-12) = 0 → 4x + 3y - 56 = 0.\n\nReflect G=(19/3, 4) across 4x+3y-56=0.\nFor line ax+by+c=0, reflection of (x0,y0):\nd = (a x0 